{
  "id": "27965_ikaru",
  "headword": "{怒|いか}る",
  "reading": "いかる",
  "romaji": "ikaru",
  "part_of_speech": "verb (godan)",
  "gloss": "to be angry; to rage",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "to be angry; to be enraged",
      "explanation": "To feel and show strong anger. A literary, somewhat dramatic word for the everyday {怒|おこ}る, often suggesting intense or dignified anger."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "27965_ikaru_ex1",
      "japanese": "{神|かみ}が{怒|いか}る。",
      "english": "The gods are angry.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "27965_ikaru_ex2",
      "japanese": "{彼|かれ}は{怒|いか}って{席|せき}を{立|た}った。",
      "english": "He rose from his seat in anger.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "27965_ikaru_ex3",
      "japanese": "{不正|ふせい}を{知|し}った{人々|ひとびと}は{激|はげ}しく{怒|いか}った。",
      "english": "The people who learned of the injustice were fiercely enraged.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    }
  ],
  "notes": "TRANSITIVITY:\n- Intransitive ({自動詞|じどうし}). The thing that angers takes に: Xに{怒|いか}る.\n- The kanji {怒|いか}る is the same as {怒|おこ}る; only the reading differs.\n\nUSAGE:\n{怒|いか}る is literary and elevated, used in writing, narration, and set expressions. In everyday conversation people say {怒|おこ}る instead. It can also evoke grand or righteous anger (of gods, nature, or a crowd).\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {怒|おこ}る: to get angry — the ordinary spoken word for the same meaning\n- {怒|いか}り: anger — the noun form, common in normal writing and speech",
